<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kül Tigin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/731">731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kul Tigin was a prince and military commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a>, brother of the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilge-qaghan">Bilge</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>. He died in 731, and his memorial inscription in the Orkhon valley is one of the earliest surviving texts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> language. His career belongs to the late phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kul-tigin.jpg" alt="Kül Tigin" width="283" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_Head_of_Koltegin_Statue_(35324303410).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
